1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,806
Aarav's bicycle lamp is glowing dim.

2
00:00:02,806 --> 00:00:05,331
He picks up a meter with two probes.

3
00:00:05,681 --> 00:00:07,259
But where do the probes go?

4
00:00:07,759 --> 00:00:10,323
In a circuit, we measure three things.

5
00:00:10,323 --> 00:00:12,426
Voltage, current and resistance.

6
00:00:12,776 --> 00:00:16,111
Each one needs the meter
connected in a different way.

7
00:00:16,461 --> 00:00:21,180
And for signals that change with time,
we use a fourth tool, the oscilloscope.

8
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:23,673
Let's start on the breadboard.

9
00:00:23,673 --> 00:00:26,428
A six volt battery, a resistor and a lamp.

10
00:00:26,778 --> 00:00:29,178
Voltage is a push between two points.

11
00:00:29,178 --> 00:00:32,455
So the voltmeter goes across
the part, in parallel.

12
00:00:32,805 --> 00:00:37,113
Red probe on the plus side of the
lamp, black probe on the other side.

13
00:00:37,113 --> 00:00:39,072
It reads four point five volts.

14
00:00:39,422 --> 00:00:42,212
A good voltmeter has a
very high resistance.

15
00:00:42,212 --> 00:00:46,232
It takes almost no current, so
it does not disturb the circuit.

16
00:00:46,882 --> 00:00:48,205
Current is different.

17
00:00:48,205 --> 00:00:52,451
Current is a flow through the wire,
so the ammeter must sit in the path,

18
00:00:52,451 --> 00:00:53,351
in series.

19
00:00:53,578 --> 00:00:57,397
Switch off, break the circuit,
and put the ammeter into the gap.

20
00:00:57,397 --> 00:00:59,730
Now every electron must pass through it.

21
00:01:00,080 --> 00:01:01,015
Switch on.

22
00:01:01,015 --> 00:01:03,497
It reads zero point three amperes.

23
00:01:03,847 --> 00:01:08,377
A good ammeter has a very low
resistance, so it hardly slows the flow.

24
00:01:09,027 --> 00:01:10,916
Resistance is the odd one.

25
00:01:10,916 --> 00:01:13,858
The ohmmeter has its
own small cell inside.

26
00:01:14,208 --> 00:01:18,630
So switch the circuit off, lift the
resistor out, and measure it alone.

27
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:19,530
Five ohms.

28
00:01:19,785 --> 00:01:22,652
Measure a live circuit,
and the reading is wrong.

29
00:01:22,652 --> 00:01:24,464
The meter can even be damaged.

30
00:01:25,114 --> 00:01:26,376
Voltmeter across.

31
00:01:26,376 --> 00:01:27,649
Ammeter in line.

32
00:01:27,649 --> 00:01:29,666
Ohmmeter with the power off.

33
00:01:30,466 --> 00:01:32,782
Now, signals that change with time.

34
00:01:32,782 --> 00:01:34,477
A meter shows one number.

35
00:01:34,477 --> 00:01:36,792
An oscilloscope draws the whole wave.

36
00:01:37,142 --> 00:01:40,259
The screen is a grid of
boxes called divisions.

37
00:01:40,259 --> 00:01:43,079
Two knobs tell you what one box is worth.

38
00:01:43,429 --> 00:01:46,848
The volts per division knob
sets the height of one box.

39
00:01:46,848 --> 00:01:49,493
The time per division knob sets its width.

40
00:01:49,843 --> 00:01:50,743
Watch.

41
00:01:50,427 --> 00:01:54,022
Turn up the signal generator,
and the wave grows taller.

42
00:01:54,022 --> 00:01:55,843
The readout climbs with it.

43
00:01:56,193 --> 00:01:59,747
Turn up the frequency, and the
waves crowd closer together.

44
00:01:59,747 --> 00:02:01,287
The period gets shorter.

45
00:02:01,937 --> 00:02:05,042
So here is how to read
any wave, step by step.

46
00:02:05,392 --> 00:02:09,052
Count the boxes from the lowest
point to the highest point.

47
00:02:09,052 --> 00:02:11,042
Multiply by volts per division.

48
00:02:11,042 --> 00:02:13,242
That is the peak to peak voltage.

49
00:02:13,942 --> 00:02:16,604
Half of that is the peak voltage, V m.

50
00:02:17,304 --> 00:02:22,148
For a sine wave, the RMS value
is the peak divided by root two.

51
00:02:22,848 --> 00:02:25,814
Now count the boxes across one full cycle.

52
00:02:25,814 --> 00:02:27,963
Multiply by time per division.

53
00:02:27,963 --> 00:02:29,797
That is the period, T.

54
00:02:30,496 --> 00:02:32,856
And the frequency is one over T.

55
00:02:32,856 --> 00:02:36,690
Voltage in volts, time in
seconds, frequency in hertz.

56
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:38,439
Worked example.

57
00:02:38,439 --> 00:02:43,489
A sine wave is four divisions high,
peak to peak, at two volts per division.

58
00:02:43,839 --> 00:02:48,330
One cycle is four divisions wide,
at five milliseconds per division.

59
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:51,571
Peak to peak is four
times two, eight volts.

60
00:02:51,571 --> 00:02:53,358
So the peak is four volts.

61
00:02:53,958 --> 00:02:59,065
The RMS value is four divided by
root two, about two point eight three

62
00:02:59,065 --> 00:02:59,965
volts.

63
00:03:00,326 --> 00:03:04,033
The period is four times
five, twenty milliseconds.

64
00:03:04,033 --> 00:03:08,156
So the frequency is one over
zero point zero two seconds.

65
00:03:08,156 --> 00:03:09,220
Fifty hertz.

66
00:03:09,870 --> 00:03:11,336
Now, the exam trap.

67
00:03:11,686 --> 00:03:15,811
Many students connect the ammeter
across a part, like a voltmeter.

68
00:03:15,811 --> 00:03:16,892
Never do that.

69
00:03:17,242 --> 00:03:19,609
An ammeter has almost no resistance.

70
00:03:19,609 --> 00:03:22,496
Across a part it acts
like a short circuit.

71
00:03:22,496 --> 00:03:26,601
A huge current flows, and the
meter or its fuse can burn out.

72
00:03:27,251 --> 00:03:28,757
Where is this asked?

73
00:03:28,757 --> 00:03:30,263
In PUC board papers.

74
00:03:30,263 --> 00:03:34,902
How to connect each meter, and how
to read voltage and frequency from an

75
00:03:34,902 --> 00:03:36,287
oscilloscope screen.

76
00:03:36,937 --> 00:03:37,837
Try this.

77
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:42,657
A sine wave on an oscilloscope is
five divisions peak to peak, at zero

78
00:03:42,657 --> 00:03:44,866
point five volts per division.

79
00:03:44,866 --> 00:03:50,338
One cycle is eight divisions wide, at
zero point one milliseconds per division.

80
00:03:50,338 --> 00:03:53,398
Find the RMS voltage and the frequency.

81
00:03:58,998 --> 00:03:59,898
A.

82
00:03:59,430 --> 00:04:04,189
About zero point eight eight volts,
and one point two five kilohertz.

83
00:04:04,539 --> 00:04:09,134
Peak to peak is five times zero
point five, two point five volts.

84
00:04:09,134 --> 00:04:12,061
So the peak is one point two five volts.

85
00:04:12,411 --> 00:04:14,000
Divide by root two.

86
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,050
The RMS value is about zero
point eight eight volts.

87
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:23,069
The period is eight times zero point
one, zero point eight milliseconds.

88
00:04:23,069 --> 00:04:26,878
One over that is one thousand
two hundred and fifty hertz.

89
00:04:27,528 --> 00:04:29,050
Screenshot this card.

90
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:36,194
Next, instrument symbols,
controls, ranges and precautions.

91
00:04:36,194 --> 00:04:39,565
What do all those markings
on a meter really mean?
